# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# clang-tidy configuration for FrostIDE (PLAN.md 6.4 step 2; ADR 0027 decision 1), read by the pinned
# clang-tidy (LLVM 23.1.4) that tools/verify_support.py runs from both verify scripts: on the changed
# files' translation units in -Quick, on every TU in -Full. Every finding fails the gate
# (WarningsAsErrors). Directories may narrow this set with their own .clang-tidy that inherits it
# (src/app and tests/app, for the frozen start-up code); a changed .clang-tidy makes tidy check every TU.
#
# The set is curated: the families that find real defects (bugprone, performance) minus the checks
# below, plus single checks from modernize, readability, misc, cppcoreguidelines, google and
# concurrency. The re-survey that chose it ran every TU of the Debug trees on both systems (104 on
# Windows, 89 on Linux) with the final set and the excluded checks switched back on; its counts are
# findings on the tree at the commit that added this file (Windows/Linux), and every finding of an
# enabled check was fixed or carries a NOLINT with its reason:
#   -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters  36/28: style; adjacent same-type parameters (two offsets, a
#       path and its contents) are the APIs' natural shape, and named types for each would not pay
#   -bugprone-signed-bitwise  154/153: flag masks; the Win32, POSIX and Qt flag constants are signed ints
#   -bugprone-unchecked-optional-access  39/51: the dataflow model does not follow the checks the code
#       makes (gtest's ASSERT_TRUE(x.has_value()), early returns), so nearly every report is a false alarm
#   -bugprone-exception-escape  32/12: noexcept functions and main call std:: code that can throw (mostly
#       allocation); the code treats std::bad_alloc as fatal, and the policy is ADR 0027's question 4
#   -bugprone-implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result  23/22: size arithmetic on small 32-bit counts
#       (pages, chunk sizes) that cannot overflow before the widening
#   -bugprone-misplaced-widening-cast  9/9: the same pattern in the perf probes and text tests
#   -bugprone-unhandled-exception-at-new  1/1: ref.hpp's allocation may throw std::bad_alloc on purpose,
#       which, as everywhere, ends the program
#   -bugprone-random-generator-seed  2/1: the tests seed their generators with constants to be reproducible
#   -bugprone-casting-through-void  7/0: GetProcAddress results become function pointers through void*,
#       the cast that clang's -Wcast-function-type-mismatch accepts; 5 of the 7 are in the frozen start-up code
#   -bugprone-multi-level-implicit-pointer-conversion  7/0: Win32 APIs take void* for pointer-to-pointer
#       arguments (LocalFree(argv), UpdateProcThreadAttribute); 1 of the 7 is in the frozen start-up code
#   -performance-enum-size  4/3: an enum's base type is left to the compiler unless a layout needs it
#   -performance-no-int-to-ptr  12/0: Win32 handles and resource IDs are integers by API contract
#   -performance-inefficient-string-concatenation  5/2: one-off messages, where clarity beats allocations
#   -performance-inefficient-vector-operation  23/24: test and setup loops of a few elements
#   (not enabled) misc-misplaced-const  16/0: `const HANDLE` is meant as the constant pointer it is
#   (not enabled) clang-analyzer-*  -/-: speed; it made single TUs 1.1 to 30 times slower (tests/io/process_test.cpp
#       2.9 s to 84.5 s), too slow for every -Quick; a later ADR may run it in -Full
# misc-confusable-identifiers is enabled: --enable-check-profile gives it about 1 ms per TU (at most
# 4 ms), and six TUs timed with and without it differ by -0.02 to +0.12 s, inside run-to-run noise.
#
# Suppressions: only `// NOLINT(<check>): <reason>` or `// NOLINTNEXTLINE(<check>): <reason>`, naming
# the check and saying why the code is right; never a bare NOLINT and never a whole-file switch.
# HeaderFilterRegex stays empty here: verify_support.py passes --header-filter for <repo>/src and
# <repo>/tests, anchored at the repository root, so generated headers under build/ stay out.
Checks: >-
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-signed-bitwise,
  -bugprone-unchecked-optional-access,
  -bugprone-exception-escape,
  -bugprone-implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result,
  -bugprone-misplaced-widening-cast,
  -bugprone-unhandled-exception-at-new,
  -bugprone-random-generator-seed,
  -bugprone-casting-through-void,
  -bugprone-multi-level-implicit-pointer-conversion,
  performance-*,
  -performance-enum-size,
  -performance-no-int-to-ptr,
  -performance-inefficient-string-concatenation,
  -performance-inefficient-vector-operation,
  modernize-use-nullptr,
  modernize-use-override,
  modernize-use-equals-default,
  modernize-use-equals-delete,
  modernize-redundant-void-arg,
  modernize-use-emplace,
  modernize-loop-convert,
  modernize-make-unique,
  modernize-make-shared,
  modernize-use-using,
  modernize-deprecated-headers,
  readability-container-size-empty,
  readability-redundant-string-cstr,
  readability-misleading-indentation,
  readability-duplicate-include,
  readability-redundant-control-flow,
  readability-redundant-smartptr-get,
  readability-static-definition-in-anonymous-namespace,
  readability-delete-null-pointer,
  readability-redundant-declaration,
  misc-redundant-expression,
  misc-unused-using-decls,
  misc-unused-alias-decls,
  misc-throw-by-value-catch-by-reference,
  misc-uniqueptr-reset-release,
  misc-definitions-in-headers,
  misc-static-assert,
  misc-misleading-identifier,
  misc-misleading-bidirectional,
  misc-confusable-identifiers,
  cppcoreguidelines-slicing,
  cppcoreguidelines-virtual-class-destructor,
  cppcoreguidelines-missing-std-forward,
  cppcoreguidelines-prefer-member-initializer,
  google-explicit-constructor,
  concurrency-thread-canceltype-asynchronous
WarningsAsErrors: '*'
HeaderFilterRegex: ''
SystemHeaders: false
FormatStyle: none
CheckOptions:
  # `(void)call()` is the codebase's explicit "result deliberately ignored".
  bugprone-unused-return-value.AllowCastToVoid: true
  # The Win32 conversion APIs take an explicit length, so a string_view's data() is safe there.
  bugprone-suspicious-stringview-data-usage.AllowedCallees: 'MultiByteToWideChar;WideCharToMultiByte'
